==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|grynden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|grindan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*grindan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*grindaną}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|gríende}}, {{m|stq|griene|t=to grind, mill}}, {{cog|nl|grinden|t=to grind|pos=rare}} and {{m|nl|grind||gravel, shingle}}, {{cog|sq|grind||to brawl, fight}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɹaɪnd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-grind.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪnd|s=1}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|grinds|grinding|ground|past2=grinded}} (''see usage notes below'')

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To reduce to [[smaller]] pieces by [[crush]]ing with [[lateral]] motion.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[shape]] with the force of [[friction]].
#: {{ux|en|'''grind''' a lens; '''grind''' an axe}}
# {{lb|en|metalworking}} To [[remove]] material by [[rub]]bing with an [[abrasive]] surface.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become ground, pulverized, or polished by friction.
#: {{ux|en|This corn '''grinds''' well.}}
#: {{ux|en|Steel '''grinds''' to a sharp edge.}}
# To move with much difficulty or friction; to [[grate]].
# {{lb|en|sports|intransitive}} To [[slide]] the flat portion of a [[skateboard]] or [[snowboard]] across an obstacle such as a railing.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[oppress]], [[hold down]] or [[weaken]].
# {{lb|en|slang|intransitive}} To [[rotate]] the [[hips]] [[erotic]]ally.
#* {{quote-song|en|title=Darling Nikki|author={{w|Prince (musician)|Prince}}|artist=Prince and the Revolution|album=w:Purple Rain|year=1984|passage=She said, "How'd you like to waste some time?" / And I could not resist when I saw little Nikki '''grind'''}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} To [[dance]] in a [[sexual]]ly suggestive way with both partners in very close proximity, often pressed against each other.
# {{lb|en|video games}} To repeat a task a large number of times in a row to achieve a specific goal.
#: {{ux|en|These enemies give lots of loot when killed, so many players fight them to '''grind''' for resources.}}
#: {{usex|en|The first level of the game is the best place to '''grind''' extra lives.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Will Luton|title=Free-to-Play: Making Money From Games You Give Away|publisher=New Riders|isbn=9780133411249|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QIXNquWvB2oC&lpg=PA32&dq=%22core%20loops%22&hl=es&pg=PA38#v=onepage&q&f=false|text=Similarly, nearly all massively multiplayer online role-playing games (MMORPGs), such as ''Dungeons & Dragons Online'', feature '''grind''': Players repeat tasks, or often “quests”, to gain in-game currency to spend on weapons or other ancillary items.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=''Evolve'' Review: Middle of the food chain|author=Steven Strom|date=Feb 14 2015|site=Ars Technica|passage=To extend the variety past that, you'll need to unlock new units in each class, meaning you have to '''grind''' through the rather lengthy process of using every one of your class’ weapons and skills significantly across several matches.|url=https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2015/02/evolve-review-middle-of-the-food-chain/}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To operate by turning a crank.
#: {{ux|en|to '''grind''' an organ}}
# To produce [[mechanical]]ly and [[repetitive]]ly as if by turning a [[crank]].
# {{lb|en|computing|dated}} To automatically [[format]] and [[indent]] code.
# {{lb|en|slang|Hawaii}} To [[eat]].
#: {{ux|en|Eh, brah, let's go '''grind'''.}}
# To instill through repetitive teaching.
#: {{ux|en|'''Grinding''' lessons into students' heads does not motivate them to learn.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To work or study hard; to [[hustle]] or [[drudge]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Grinding''' Leetcode}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1862|author=Frederic W. Farrar|title=St. Winifred's: or the World of School|text=One evening, during evening work, Charlie was trying hard to do the verses which had been set to his form. {{...}} Wilton, whose conduct had been more impertinent than that of any one else, said to Charlie— <br>“I say, young Evson, how you are '''grinding'''.” <br>“I have these verses to do,” said Charlie simply.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[annoy]] or [[irritate]] (a person); to [[grind one's gears]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Steven Wunderink|title=Minding Your Spiritual Business: Life Stories with Life Sense|page=139|passage=I need to pontificate on something that really '''grinds''' me. So here goes. I am sick and tired of lazy thinkers using the defense of “legislated morality.”}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In the sports and video game senses, the past participle and past tense form {{m|en|grinded}} is often used instead of the irregular form {{m|en|ground}}.
* Historically, there also existed a past participle form {{m|en|grounden}}, but it is now archaic or obsolete.
* When used to denote sexually suggestive dancing between two partners, the past participle and past tense form {{m|en|grinded}} is almost always used.

